`interpgrid` defines `Ny, Nx` first, then takes one of two branches. In the array-input branch it defines `x`, `y`, `xn`, and `yn` from `xi`/`yi`; in the scalar branch it defines `x`, `y`, then defines `xn` and `yn` through either the boundary assignment or the increment assignment. The common tail then reads those values to compute `a00`, `a01`, `a10`, `a11`, `xt`, `yt`, `a0`, `a1`, and `ai`; finally `ai` is read in the scalar-only masked check and again at `return ai`. Because parameters are not counted as definitions, `a`, `xi`, and `yi` produce no def-use pairs.

